1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,675
Saint John's Island: The Quarantine Island

2
00:00:03,675 --> 00:00:08,281
For more than a century, almost
every stranger who came to Singapore

3
00:00:08,361 --> 00:00:12,415
by sea began their Singapore
story on the same small island,

4
00:00:12,495 --> 00:00:17,250
six and a half kilometres south of
the mainland: Saint John's Island.

5
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:21,563
Migrants, pilgrims, traders and
troops were landed there first,

6
00:00:21,643 --> 00:00:24,632
examined, disinfected,
vaccinated and held —

7
00:00:24,712 --> 00:00:27,770
sometimes for two days,
sometimes for weeks —

8
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:31,825
before they were allowed to
become part of the port city.

9
00:00:31,825 --> 00:00:34,957
At its height the island's
quarantine station

10
00:00:35,037 --> 00:00:37,750
was the largest in the British Empire.

11
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:40,819
Today the same island
holds a holiday lodge,

12
00:00:40,899 --> 00:00:45,042
two marine laboratories and a
small, ageing colony of cats,

13
00:00:45,122 --> 00:00:50,275
and most Singaporeans know it, if at
all, as a quiet place for a picnic.

14
00:00:50,275 --> 00:00:52,895
This is the story of the island that

15
00:00:52,975 --> 00:00:55,375
stood guard at Singapore's door.

16
00:00:55,375 --> 00:01:00,150
Saint John's was Singapore's
doorstep before it was anything else.

17
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:03,723
On the 28th of January 1819,
Stamford Raffles' fleet

18
00:01:03,803 --> 00:01:08,078
anchored off the island, and
representatives of the Temenggong

19
00:01:08,158 --> 00:01:11,872
of Johor came out to the
ships and assured the British

20
00:01:11,952 --> 00:01:15,675
that there were no Dutch
settlers on the main island.

21
00:01:15,675 --> 00:01:19,883
Only the next day, the 29th
of January, did Raffles land

22
00:01:19,963 --> 00:01:24,175
on Singapore itself and begin
the founding of the port.

23
00:01:24,175 --> 00:01:27,071
For its first decades the island served

24
00:01:27,151 --> 00:01:29,975
the new port as an outer signal post.

25
00:01:29,975 --> 00:01:34,018
Raffles' instructions of
February 1819 ordered a European

26
00:01:34,098 --> 00:01:37,997
stationed on Saint John's
with a boat and a small crew,

27
00:01:38,077 --> 00:01:42,338
to board square-sailed vessels
passing through the Straits —

28
00:01:42,418 --> 00:01:46,975
in effect, heralds announcing
the new port to passing captains.

29
00:01:46,975 --> 00:01:51,227
A signal flagstaff, moved
from Pulau Tambakul (today's

30
00:01:51,307 --> 00:01:55,479
Kusu Island) in 1823, stood
on the island until 1833,

31
00:01:55,559 --> 00:01:59,410
and the flag survives in
the island's Malay name:

32
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:04,625
Pulau Sekijang Bendera, bendera
being the Malay word for a flag.

33
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:08,667
Its neighbour, Lazarus Island,
is Pulau Sekijang Pelepah,

34
00:02:08,747 --> 00:02:13,656
after its palm fronds; both began
as "Sekijang", barking-deer island,

35
00:02:13,736 --> 00:02:18,075
a name the European charts
gradually bent into "Saint John".

36
00:02:18,075 --> 00:02:22,054
When the signal station
was abandoned in 1834,

37
00:02:22,134 --> 00:02:24,075
the island went quiet.

38
00:02:24,075 --> 00:02:28,341
A visiting naturalist that
year found a single old Malay

39
00:02:28,421 --> 00:02:33,000
inhabitant among coconut,
orange, guava and plantain trees.

40
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,550
It would not stay quiet for long.

41
00:02:35,550 --> 00:02:39,194
The legal groundwork came
in 1868, when the Quarantine

42
00:02:39,274 --> 00:02:44,436
Ordinance gave the port its first real
powers: a ship arriving with cholera,

43
00:02:44,516 --> 00:02:47,608
smallpox or fever aboard
had to anchor outside

44
00:02:47,688 --> 00:02:51,263
the harbour limits, hoist
the yellow quarantine flag,

45
00:02:51,343 --> 00:02:55,550
and let nobody board or leave
until it was medically cleared.

46
00:02:55,550 --> 00:03:00,425
Merchants grumbled for years that
the rules interfered with trade.

47
00:03:00,425 --> 00:03:04,734
Then, in 1873, cholera
arrived from Bangkok despite

48
00:03:04,814 --> 00:03:08,176
an order quarantining vessels from Siam,

49
00:03:08,256 --> 00:03:12,995
and between July and September
there were 857 cases and,

50
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:17,550
by the National Library
Board's account, 448 deaths.

51
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:21,175
Shipboard orders, it turned
out, were not enough.

52
00:03:21,175 --> 00:03:24,750
Singapore needed a
place to put the people.

53
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:28,922
The plan came from the Acting
Master Attendant, Henry Ellis:

54
00:03:29,002 --> 00:03:34,380
a lazaretto — a quarantine station — on
Saint John's Island, with a hospital,

55
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:38,925
a patrol boat and a burial ground
on Peak Island, today's Kusu.

56
00:03:38,925 --> 00:03:43,374
It was approved and announced
in the Governor's speech

57
00:03:43,454 --> 00:03:47,900
to the Legislative Council
on the 21st of March 1874.

58
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:51,275
The station opened that
November, barely finished,

59
00:03:51,355 --> 00:03:55,560
and was tested at once: its first
intake was the passengers of

60
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:59,499
the cholera-stricken S.S.
Milton, out of Swatow and bound

61
00:03:59,579 --> 00:04:03,231
for Penang — over a thousand
Chinese passengers landed

62
00:04:03,311 --> 00:04:07,250
on an island that had, months
before, held almost nobody.

63
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:12,550
One part of Ellis's plan did not
survive contact with the community.

64
00:04:12,550 --> 00:04:16,637
Burials began on Kusu, but
in 1875 the community leader

65
00:04:16,717 --> 00:04:21,183
Cheang Hong Lim objected in
writing that the island had been

66
00:04:21,263 --> 00:04:26,563
used for over thirty years for Chinese
and Malay religious observances,

67
00:04:26,643 --> 00:04:29,825
and had been desecrated by the interments.

68
00:04:29,825 --> 00:04:32,087
From the early twentieth century,

69
00:04:32,167 --> 00:04:37,978
passengers who died on arrival or soon
after were buried instead on Lazarus Island,

70
00:04:38,058 --> 00:04:40,400
which only officials could visit.

71
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,650
The routine hardened into a system.

72
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:47,041
A ship classed as "infected"
— a case of disease

73
00:04:47,121 --> 00:04:49,571
within twelve days before arrival —

74
00:04:49,651 --> 00:04:53,700
or merely "suspected" sent
its passengers to the island.

75
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:58,586
Steerage passengers landed at the
jetty and passed through muster sheds,

76
00:04:58,666 --> 00:05:02,242
where they changed while
their clothes and belongings

77
00:05:02,322 --> 00:05:06,450
were steamed in boiler houses;
by 1930 they were also sprayed

78
00:05:06,530 --> 00:05:10,875
with Izal disinfectant in
purpose-built disinfection buildings.

79
00:05:10,875 --> 00:05:14,438
The ships themselves were
fumigated with sulphur,

80
00:05:14,518 --> 00:05:16,525
stored loose on the island.

81
00:05:16,525 --> 00:05:20,763
Then came the camps: rows of
accommodation with their own kitchens,

82
00:05:20,843 --> 00:05:24,179
a vaccination for everyone,
and a wait — two or three

83
00:05:24,259 --> 00:05:28,754
days for most deck passengers, a week
or more for migrants from China —

84
00:05:28,834 --> 00:05:32,250
until the doctors cleared
the batch for the mainland.

85
00:05:32,250 --> 00:05:35,675
Quarantine, though, ran on a class system.

86
00:05:35,675 --> 00:05:38,705
First- and second-class
cabin passengers simply

87
00:05:38,785 --> 00:05:42,213
presented themselves for
clearance and walked ashore;

88
00:05:42,293 --> 00:05:45,852
it was the steerage passengers,
the ones who had shared

89
00:05:45,932 --> 00:05:49,175
a deck or a hold, who were
ferried to the island.

90
00:05:49,175 --> 00:05:52,033
The station served others
besides immigrants:

91
00:05:52,113 --> 00:05:56,865
returning Haj pilgrims were routinely
processed there too, and in 1890 the

92
00:05:56,945 --> 00:06:01,175
pilgrims of the steamer Queen
Margaret, homeward bound from Mecca,

93
00:06:01,255 --> 00:06:04,244
were quarantined on the
Deputy Health Officer's

94
00:06:04,324 --> 00:06:08,227
instruction because cholera
deaths had occurred aboard during

95
00:06:08,307 --> 00:06:12,225
the voyage — even though no
cases remained when she arrived.

96
00:06:12,225 --> 00:06:16,660
The diseases that kept the island
busy changed with the decades:

97
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:20,118
cholera first, then smallpox
— the Singapore Free

98
00:06:20,198 --> 00:06:24,069
Press noted in 1926 that
smallpox appeared on two out of

99
00:06:24,149 --> 00:06:27,455
every four immigrant ships
— and bubonic plague,

100
00:06:27,535 --> 00:06:33,170
which prompted a plague hospital on the
island after the 1894 Hong Kong outbreak;

101
00:06:33,250 --> 00:06:36,425
a plague case was caught
there in March 1896.

102
00:06:36,425 --> 00:06:41,200
Chickenpox, measles and beri-beri
all passed through as well.

103
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:46,900
What began in 1874 as a cluster
of attap huts grew steadily:

104
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:48,730
a hospital in 1894,

105
00:06:48,810 --> 00:06:53,161
more than $300,000 of
redevelopment from 1903,

106
00:06:53,241 --> 00:06:58,250
and by 1908 a station that
covered the whole island.

107
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:01,880
An inventory published in The
Straits Times in 1935 gives the

108
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:05,165
measure of the place: hospitals
for smallpox, cholera,

109
00:07:05,245 --> 00:07:08,996
plague and chickenpox; muster
sheds and disinfection buildings;

110
00:07:09,076 --> 00:07:13,254
sulphur storehouses; a dispensary;
a police station, a Coroner's court

111
00:07:13,334 --> 00:07:17,268
and a lock-up; barracks for fifteen
Sikh policemen, with a temple;

112
00:07:17,348 --> 00:07:20,552
a mosque; quarters for
gardeners, coolies and workmen;

113
00:07:20,632 --> 00:07:24,525
a post office; and telephone wires
tying it all to the mainland.

114
00:07:24,525 --> 00:07:29,709
By the mid-1920s the station
could hold 6,000 people

115
00:07:29,789 --> 00:07:34,750
at any one time, organised
by 1935 into 22 camps.

116
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:40,058
Between 1903 and 1923, by the
Singapore Free Press's account,

117
00:07:40,138 --> 00:07:42,443
it inspected some 8 million

118
00:07:42,523 --> 00:07:46,675
arrivals and quarantined
about 300,000 of them.

119
00:07:46,675 --> 00:07:51,552
A model of the station was
exhibited in the Malaya Pavilion

120
00:07:51,632 --> 00:07:56,000
at the British Empire
Exhibition at Wembley in 1924.

121
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,944
The National Library Board
describes it as the largest

122
00:08:00,024 --> 00:08:02,999
quarantine station in the British Empire;

123
00:08:03,079 --> 00:08:07,469
The Straits Times in 1935 ranked
it the largest in the world

124
00:08:07,549 --> 00:08:11,275
after New York's Ellis
Island and El Tor in Egypt.

125
00:08:11,275 --> 00:08:13,925
The official view was sunny.

126
00:08:13,925 --> 00:08:18,823
The Free Press in 1926 assured its
readers that immigrants "arrive hungry,

127
00:08:18,903 --> 00:08:22,725
dirty and miserable after a
deck passage through the China

128
00:08:22,805 --> 00:08:26,626
Sea and they spend five blissful
days… with nothing to do,

129
00:08:26,706 --> 00:08:31,550
wholesome food to eat and the beaches
of the island on which to lounge".

130
00:08:31,550 --> 00:08:34,106
The migrants' own recorded memories,

131
00:08:34,186 --> 00:08:38,725
preserved in National Archives
oral histories, are more mixed.

132
00:08:38,725 --> 00:08:45,041
Teo Choon Hong, who arrived from Amoy in
1937, remembered the class divide bitterly:

133
00:08:45,121 --> 00:08:48,700
"Only those on the lower
deck were quarantined.

134
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:52,525
Those who stayed in the
cabins did not have to go.

135
00:08:52,525 --> 00:08:54,950
There was a class distinction… We were

136
00:08:55,030 --> 00:08:57,600
bossed around like chickens and ducks."

137
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:01,950
He was held two days, cooking
his own rations in the camp.

138
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:07,768
Teong Eng Siong, arriving from Foochow in
1948, remembered the same system mildly:

139
00:09:07,848 --> 00:09:10,573
three to five days, three meals a day —

140
00:09:10,653 --> 00:09:14,816
two small slices of bread and
milk tea for breakfast, eggs,

141
00:09:14,896 --> 00:09:20,650
vegetables and fish later — two showers
a day and a big shared hall to sleep in.

142
00:09:20,650 --> 00:09:24,402
And Saravana Perumal, who
came from Jaffna in 1947,

143
00:09:24,482 --> 00:09:29,737
recalled being "locked up in the
camp" with rations, firewood and pots,

144
00:09:29,817 --> 00:09:33,950
and being frightened by the
centipedes and cockroaches.

145
00:09:33,950 --> 00:09:38,151
Perumal's story has a second
act: in 1955 he returned

146
00:09:38,231 --> 00:09:41,140
to Saint John's as a member of staff,

147
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:45,825
transferred to help set up
the island's next institution.

148
00:09:45,825 --> 00:09:49,675
Quarantine was never
the island's only use.

149
00:09:49,675 --> 00:09:54,239
In the First World War, most
German men in Singapore were

150
00:09:54,319 --> 00:09:59,045
interned on Saint John's; by
1916, 296 enemy nationals from

151
00:09:59,125 --> 00:10:03,850
the island had been shipped
on to internment in Australia.

152
00:10:03,850 --> 00:10:07,026
In the Second World War
the pattern repeated:

153
00:10:07,106 --> 00:10:11,874
enemy nationals — some of them
refugees who had fled Nazi Germany —

154
00:10:11,954 --> 00:10:15,925
were interned there in 1940,
Japanese women and children

155
00:10:16,005 --> 00:10:18,747
were held on the island from late 1941,

156
00:10:18,827 --> 00:10:23,675
and Allied prisoners of war were
detained there after the invasion.

157
00:10:23,675 --> 00:10:27,311
A Chinese-style moon gate
built by German internees

158
00:10:27,391 --> 00:10:30,525
still stands on the
island's western shore.

159
00:10:30,525 --> 00:10:35,308
When the Malayan Emergency was
declared in 1948, the island was

160
00:10:35,388 --> 00:10:39,168
fitted out as a detention
centre: the first batch,

161
00:10:39,248 --> 00:10:45,343
200 political prisoners from Johor Bahru,
arrived on the 20th of September 1948,

162
00:10:45,423 --> 00:10:49,900
and within two years more than
600 people were held there.

163
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:53,766
Among the detainees of the
1950s was C. V. Devan Nair,

164
00:10:53,846 --> 00:10:58,369
held for anti-colonial activities,
who called the island "Saint

165
00:10:58,449 --> 00:11:01,876
John's University" for
the reading he did there;

166
00:11:01,956 --> 00:11:06,925
a young lawyer named Lee Kuan Yew
visited him on the island in 1952.

167
00:11:06,925 --> 00:11:10,818
In Operation Coldstore, on
the 2nd of February 1963,

168
00:11:10,898 --> 00:11:16,548
113 left-wing politicians and trade
unionists were arrested, and 99 of them

169
00:11:16,628 --> 00:11:21,208
were moved to Saint John's.
Nair, released and detained again

170
00:11:21,288 --> 00:11:26,025
over the years, went on to become
Singapore's third President.

171
00:11:26,025 --> 00:11:28,558
The island's last great institution

172
00:11:28,638 --> 00:11:31,325
treated a different kind of arrival.

173
00:11:31,325 --> 00:11:36,987
Post-war Singapore counted
more than 16,000 opium addicts;

174
00:11:37,067 --> 00:11:40,750
opium had been banned in October 1945,

175
00:11:40,830 --> 00:11:46,275
yet over 1,400 opium dens
were still operating in 1949.

176
00:11:46,275 --> 00:11:51,280
In February 1955 the colonial
government opened the Opium Treatment

177
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:55,833
Centre on Saint John's — its
first attempt at rehabilitation

178
00:11:55,913 --> 00:12:01,150
instead of imprisonment — run,
strikingly, by the Prisons Department.

179
00:12:01,150 --> 00:12:06,706
The regime ran in phases: withdrawal over
two to four weeks in the prison hospital,

180
00:12:06,786 --> 00:12:09,829
the drug tapered rather
than stopped outright;

181
00:12:09,909 --> 00:12:13,767
then six months to a year of
rehabilitation on the island,

182
00:12:13,847 --> 00:12:19,075
learning carpentry, canework, tailoring
and gardening; then follow-up ashore.

183
00:12:19,075 --> 00:12:21,401
By 1966 more than 4,000

184
00:12:21,481 --> 00:12:24,725
addicts had been treated there.

185
00:12:24,725 --> 00:12:29,304
In 1973 the centre was
converted into Singapore's first

186
00:12:29,384 --> 00:12:33,793
Drug Rehabilitation Centre,
for addicts of all drugs,

187
00:12:33,873 --> 00:12:39,125
and in 1975 it closed, the island
earmarked for redevelopment.

188
00:12:39,125 --> 00:12:43,794
That same year, from May to
October, the emptied island took in

189
00:12:43,874 --> 00:12:49,000
South Vietnamese refugees — its
last intake of strangers in transit.

190
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,375
Quarantine itself died by stages,

191
00:12:51,455 --> 00:12:55,250
killed by the aeroplane
and the health certificate.

192
00:12:55,250 --> 00:12:59,448
Deck passengers from China
were exempted in 1971

193
00:12:59,528 --> 00:13:02,923
if they held valid health certificates;

194
00:13:03,003 --> 00:13:08,350
compulsory quarantine for
arrivals from India ended in 1973.

195
00:13:08,350 --> 00:13:12,959
On the 14th of January 1976,
just over a century after

196
00:13:13,039 --> 00:13:18,075
the S.S. Milton, the quarantine
station officially closed.

197
00:13:18,075 --> 00:13:23,093
The islanders — staff families
and villagers across Saint John's,

198
00:13:23,173 --> 00:13:26,622
Lazarus and Seringat,
more than 400 people in

199
00:13:26,702 --> 00:13:31,799
the early 1960s — were relocated
to the mainland in 1976 and 1977,

200
00:13:31,879 --> 00:13:34,857
and the island's little primary school,

201
00:13:34,937 --> 00:13:39,800
which had taught the staff's
children since the 1950s, closed.

202
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:43,560
A handful of residents
stayed on for decades;

203
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:47,650
the last permanent
islanders left only in 2017.

204
00:13:47,650 --> 00:13:51,915
In 1998 and 1999 a temporary
detention centre was built

205
00:13:51,995 --> 00:13:56,497
on the island for an expected
influx of illegal immigrants

206
00:13:56,577 --> 00:14:01,000
and refugees that never came;
it stood unused, a fence in

207
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:05,425
search of a purpose, and the
island settled into quiet.

208
00:14:05,425 --> 00:14:10,139
The afterlife began the same
year quarantine ended: in 1976 a

209
00:14:10,219 --> 00:14:15,955
subsidised holiday camp opened on the
island, and grander resort schemes —

210
00:14:16,035 --> 00:14:20,750
restaurants, a golf course,
even a casino — came to nothing.

211
00:14:20,750 --> 00:14:24,852
Today the overnight option is
the Saint John's Island Lodge,

212
00:14:24,932 --> 00:14:29,243
run by the Singapore Land Authority
in the island's old fabric:

213
00:14:29,323 --> 00:14:34,550
the former holiday bungalow and, under
new names, the old camps themselves.

214
00:14:34,550 --> 00:14:38,140
Day visitors come on the
ferries from Marina South Pier,

215
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:43,056
about half an hour out; there are no
shops, and everyone leaves on the last

216
00:14:43,136 --> 00:14:47,513
boat. (At the time of writing, the
island's own jetty is temporarily

217
00:14:47,593 --> 00:14:52,050
closed and ferries land at Seringat
jetty, a half-hour's walk away.)

218
00:14:52,050 --> 00:14:56,151
In 2006, reclamation and
causeways completed the linking

219
00:14:56,231 --> 00:15:00,556
of Saint John's to Lazarus and
Seringat, and the quarantine

220
00:15:00,636 --> 00:15:04,364
island became a beach
island: you can now walk from

221
00:15:04,444 --> 00:15:08,625
the old station site across
to Lazarus Island's lagoons.

222
00:15:08,625 --> 00:15:11,500
Science moved into the quiet.

223
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:15,550
The Tropical Marine Science
Institute opened its island

224
00:15:15,630 --> 00:15:19,379
laboratory in 2002; upgraded
with national funding,

225
00:15:19,459 --> 00:15:25,236
it was launched in 2016 as the Saint
John's Island National Marine Laboratory,

226
00:15:25,316 --> 00:15:29,066
Singapore's only offshore
marine research facility,

227
00:15:29,146 --> 00:15:32,750
open to researchers from
all local institutions.

228
00:15:32,750 --> 00:15:37,735
Next door, the Marine Aquaculture
Centre, established in 2003,

229
00:15:37,815 --> 00:15:41,900
works on breeding food
fish such as Asian seabass.

230
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:46,006
Since 2014 the island's western
reefs have lain inside the

231
00:15:46,086 --> 00:15:50,409
Sisters' Islands Marine Park,
Singapore's first, and a second

232
00:15:50,489 --> 00:15:55,317
marine park has been announced for
the waters off Lazarus and Kusu —

233
00:15:55,397 --> 00:15:59,150
announced, as of this
writing, but not yet gazetted.

234
00:15:59,150 --> 00:16:01,725
And then there are the cats.

235
00:16:01,725 --> 00:16:06,021
Saint John's became locally
famous as a cat island:

236
00:16:06,101 --> 00:16:11,250
in 2014 it held over a hundred
cats in five or six colonies.

237
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:15,353
The SPCA had begun a
sterilisation programme in 2011,

238
00:16:15,433 --> 00:16:19,063
and the population has
since aged and thinned —

239
00:16:19,143 --> 00:16:23,404
about sixteen cats by 2018
— cared for now by volunteer

240
00:16:23,484 --> 00:16:27,825
feeders who raise money for
the old animals' vet bills.

241
00:16:27,825 --> 00:16:32,530
The island that once processed eight
million arrivals ends its story,

242
00:16:32,610 --> 00:16:35,998
for now, as the charge of
a few dozen researchers,

243
00:16:36,078 --> 00:16:40,100
some weekend picnickers and a
small pride of elderly cats.

244
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:44,681
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Singapore's island posts usually

245
00:16:44,761 --> 00:16:49,075
face outward — Semakau the rubbish
island, Bukom the refinery.

246
00:16:49,075 --> 00:16:53,008
Saint John's faced inward: it
was the filter through which

247
00:16:53,088 --> 00:16:56,607
the port's people had to
pass, and for a century the

248
00:16:56,687 --> 00:17:00,205
first Singapore that newcomers
ever saw was a jetty,

249
00:17:00,285 --> 00:17:04,575
a disinfection shed and a camp
bed on a small island offshore.

250
00:17:04,575 --> 00:17:08,225
The door has closed; the doorstep remains.
